@using System.Globalization
@using XgFilter_Lib.Enums
@using XgFilter_Lib.Filtering
@using XgFilter_Razor.Components.Internal

@* ── FilterHelp.razor ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   Producer-owned documentation for everything FilterPanel offers — every
   facet, and the panel chrome that governs them all — in user-level
   language, behavior only, never FilterConfig internals or field names. It
   lives beside the panel it documents so the prose has one owner: consumers
   embed this component (BgQuiz's Help adds only app-level framing around it)
   and must never write their own facet or chrome prose — a second
   description of the panel's semantics is a second encoding that silently
   drifts.

   Render-only: it issues no JS interop and touches no storage or state of its
   own. It does *document* what FilterPanel persists (the "what the panel
   remembers" section) — but only by rendering the panel's own key constants,
   never by reading or writing them.

   Structured for embedding — one section per topic, each heading carrying a
   stable fh-* anchor id rendered from the named constants below (never a
   re-typed literal), facet headings from the lib's FilterFacet
   [Description]s via ToLabel() so help titles, panel section headings, and
   the hidden-active signal all name a facet identically. The shelved facets
   (Position types / Play types) are documented only when their UI returns.
   One section's identity is a consumer contract rather than internal
   structure: the storage section's id and heading text are public, because a
   host's data-ownership copy links into it — see their docs.
   Heading depth is the host's to state (HeadingLevel): only the host knows
   what outline this block is being embedded into, and a hard-coded pair
   silently malforms it — see the parameter's docs.
   Location: XgFilter_Razor/Components/FilterHelp.razor
──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── *@

<div class="filter-help">

    @Heading(HeadingLevel, LeadAnchorId, "Filters")
    <p>
        Filters select which analyzed decisions are admitted. Every filter you
        set must agree for a decision to pass — the facets combine with AND.
        Within a single facet, checked options combine with OR: checking more
        options admits more decisions. A facet left at rest (nothing typed,
        nothing checked) admits everything, so an empty panel passes every
        decision.
    </p>

    @* Panel chrome — the controls that govern every facet rather than
       describing one. Producer-owned for the same reason the facet prose is:
       the disclosure's hidden-active signal, the Apply gate and its two
       disabled states are the panel's behavior, so a host restating them
       would be a second encoding that drifts. Placed before the facets: it is
       the frame a reader needs in order to find and commit any of them. *@
    <section>
        @Heading(SectionLevel, ChromeSectionAnchorId, "Setting and applying filters")
        <p>
            Only <em>Error range</em> is shown at first — it is the one most
            people reach for. <em>Show more filters</em> opens the rest. While
            it is closed, a badge on it counts the hidden sections that
            currently hold a filter and names them, so a filter you set earlier
            can never be quietly out of sight.
        </p>
        <p>
            Nothing you type or check takes effect until you press <em>Apply
            Filter</em>. Apply is unavailable when there is nothing to apply —
            you have not changed anything since you last applied, and the panel
            says so under the button — and when some value is not usable as a
            filter. In that second case the offending box is marked and says
            what is wrong with it, right where you typed it; fix or clear it
            and Apply comes back. Nothing is guessed at or quietly ignored, so
            a filter you meant to set can never be silently dropped.
        </p>
        <p>
            <em>Clear filters</em> empties every filter and applies that
            immediately, giving you the unfiltered set back in one gesture. It
            changes only the filters — whether <em>Show more filters</em> is
            open is left exactly as you had it.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section>
        @Heading(SectionLevel, ErrorRangeAnchorId, FilterFacet.ErrorRange.ToLabel())
        <p>
            Admits decisions whose error size falls between the two bounds,
            inclusive. Leave a bound blank for no limit on that side. While an
            error range is set, decisions without a measured error are
            excluded.
        </p>
        <p>
            An error is a size, never a direction, so each bound must be a
            number of zero or more — zero itself is allowed, and means an
            exactly-perfect play. The lower bound also cannot be above the
            upper. Either mistake describes a range nothing could ever fall
            into, so it is refused outright rather than applied as a filter
            that silently admits nothing.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section>
        @Heading(SectionLevel, PlayersAnchorId, FilterFacet.Players.ToLabel())
        <p>
            Admits decisions made by any of the listed players
            (comma-separated). Blank means all players. A match involving none
            of the listed players is skipped entirely.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section>
        @Heading(SectionLevel, DecisionTypeAnchorId, FilterFacet.DecisionType.ToLabel())
        <p>
            Restricts to checker plays only, cube decisions only, or admits
            both.
        </p>
    </section>

    @* The token spellings here render from MatchScoreToken's exported
       constants, never as literals — the same discipline the storage section
       keeps with the panel's key names, and the reason those constants are
       exported at all. The section teaches the two rule-bearing money tokens
       that replaced the bare one (halheinrich/backgammon#121); the bare
       spelling is retired vocabulary and is deliberately not offered here.
       Explaining the retirement belongs to the panel, which meets it where a
       user still has one typed. *@
    <section>
        @Heading(SectionLevel, MatchScoresAnchorId, FilterFacet.MatchScores.ToLabel())
        <p>
            Admits decisions taken at any of the listed scores
            (comma-separated). Scores are written <em>MaNa</em> and are
            anchored to the player on roll: M is the points the player on roll
            still needs, N the opponent — so 4a5a and 5a4a are different
            scores; list both to catch either orientation. Append C for a
            Crawford game (1a2aC), write @MatchScoreToken.MoneyWithJacoby or
            @MatchScoreToken.MoneyWithoutJacoby for a session played with or
            without the Jacoby rule, and note double match point is 1a1a.
            Those two are separate scores like any other, so list both to
            admit a session under either rule. A session whose file never
            recorded which way the Jacoby rule was set matches neither of
            them — vanishingly rare, and nothing you can type picks it up.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section>
        @Heading(SectionLevel, MoveNumberRangeAnchorId, FilterFacet.MoveNumberRange.ToLabel())
        <p>
            Admits decisions by their move number within the game, between the
            two bounds, inclusive. Leave a bound blank for no limit on that
            side. Games that do not begin from the standard starting position
            are excluded while this filter is active.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section>
        @Heading(SectionLevel, ContactTypesAnchorId, FilterFacet.ContactTypes.ToLabel())
        <p>
            Admits decisions by the character of the position — contact or
            race. Nothing checked means all types.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section>
        @Heading(SectionLevel, AnalysisDepthAnchorId, FilterFacet.AnalysisDepth.ToLabel())
        <p>
            Admits decisions by how they were analyzed. There are three modes:
            an <strong>evaluation</strong> (XG's neural-net judgment at some
            ply level), a <strong>rollout</strong> (games played out to
            settle the decision), and a <strong>book rollout</strong> (a
            stored result looked up from XG's opening book).
        </p>
        <p>
            Check the modes you want to see. A decision passes if it was
            analyzed in any mode you checked, so checking a second mode shows
            more decisions, never fewer. Check nothing and this filter does
            nothing: every decision passes, including older ones whose
            analysis depth was never recorded. Check any mode and those
            unrecorded decisions stop passing — there is no recorded mode to
            match them against.
        </p>
        <p>
            Under each checked mode you can narrow by analysis level. Levels
            you check narrow only their own mode; none checked means any level
            for that mode. What "level" means differs by mode: an
            evaluation's level is its own; a rollout's level is the
            <em>inner</em> level its games were played at — checker plays
            never carry XG Roller-family inner levels; a book rollout's level
            is the inner level from the opening-book entry, when the book
            recorded one.
        </p>
        <p>
            Each checked mode keeps its levels behind its own <em>Analysis
            level</em> link, closed until you open it. The badge on that link
            says what is inside without your having to open it:
            <strong>any</strong> when no level is checked — that mode is not
            narrowed at all — or <strong>N selected</strong> when it is.
            Unchecking a mode hides its levels but keeps them: levels under an
            unchecked mode do nothing whatever, so checking the mode again
            brings your selection back rather than making you rebuild it.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section>
        @Heading(SectionLevel, DiceRollsAnchorId, FilterFacet.DiceRolls.ToLabel())
        <p>
            Admits checker plays made with any of the checked rolls. Dice
            order does not matter — 31 covers 3-1 and 1-3 alike. Nothing
            checked means all rolls. While rolls are checked, cube decisions
            never match: no roll exists before the cube is offered.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section>
        @Heading(SectionLevel, PositionPatternAnchorId, FilterFacet.PositionPattern.ToLabel())
        <p>
            Admits decisions whose position matches a set of per-location
            checker-count ranges, written as a bracket list —
            [location,min,max] per location, with a blank bound meaning
            unbounded. Positive counts belong to the player on roll, negative
            to the opponent. Locations are points 1–24 (numbered from the
            player on roll's perspective), the bars (25 for the player on
            roll, 0 for the opponent), and the borne-off trays (off and
            opp-off). Every listed location must match. Blank means no
            pattern filter.
        </p>
    </section>

    @* Storage copy — the two keys are rendered from FilterPanel's own internal
       constants, never written here as prose literals, so the names a reader
       verifies in devtools cannot drift from the names the panel actually
       writes. Scope is exactly what FilterPanel itself persists: a sibling key
       belonging to a host app is that host's to document, not this panel's. *@
    <section>
        @Heading(SectionLevel, StorageSectionAnchorId, StorageSectionHeading)
        <p>
            The panel remembers your filter settings so they are still there
            next time. It saves them in your own browser, on this machine, and
            nowhere else — nothing is uploaded. What is saved is the filter
            settings themselves, not your positions, your matches, or the files
            you analyzed.
        </p>
        <p>
            There are two saved entries, both kept in your browser's local
            storage for this site. They survive reloading the page and closing
            the browser, and they are tied to this browser and this site — so
            another browser, or another machine, starts fresh:
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <code>@FilterPanel.ConfigKey</code> — the filters you last
                applied. Written when you press Apply Filter and when you press
                Clear filters, and read back when the panel first appears.
            </li>
            <li>
                <code>@FilterPanel.DisclosureKey</code> — whether
                <em>Show more filters</em> is open. That is a display
                preference, kept apart from your filters: it is not part of the
                saved filter settings, and Clear filters never changes it.
            </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
            You can see both entries under local storage for this site in your
            browser's developer tools, and delete them there whenever you like.
        </p>
    </section>

</div>

@code {
    // ── Section identities ────────────────────────────────────────────────
    // Every heading's anchor id, named once here and rendered from these
    // constants below — the markup never re-types an fh-* literal, so a
    // section's id has one source and cannot drift between the markup, the
    // pins, and anything that links to it.
    //
    // Eleven of the twelve are internal: they are the block's own internal
    // structure, and freezing all of it as a consumer contract would make
    // splitting or resequencing a section a breaking change for hosts that
    // never linked to it. The storage section's pair is the exception and is
    // public — see its docs.

    // The block's lead heading ("Filters"), the one at HeadingLevel.
    internal const string LeadAnchorId = "fh-filters";

    // The chrome section — the controls that govern every facet.
    internal const string ChromeSectionAnchorId = "fh-using-the-panel";

    // One per offered facet, named for the FilterFacet member whose ToLabel()
    // supplies the heading text. The shelved facets have none: they are
    // undocumented until their UI returns.
    internal const string ErrorRangeAnchorId = "fh-error-range";
    internal const string PlayersAnchorId = "fh-players";
    internal const string DecisionTypeAnchorId = "fh-decision-type";
    internal const string MatchScoresAnchorId = "fh-match-scores";
    internal const string MoveNumberRangeAnchorId = "fh-move-number-range";
    internal const string ContactTypesAnchorId = "fh-contact-type";
    internal const string AnalysisDepthAnchorId = "fh-analysis-depth";
    internal const string DiceRollsAnchorId = "fh-dice-rolls";
    internal const string PositionPatternAnchorId = "fh-position-pattern";

    /// <summary>
    /// The anchor id of the storage-assurance section — the block's
    /// <b>host deep-link surface</b>, and the only section identity this
    /// component exports.
    /// <para>
    /// A host with its own data-ownership copy states what <i>it</i> owns and
    /// points here for the panel's half rather than restating it (the
    /// one-owner rule the whole component exists for). That promise used to
    /// live only in prose, which left the host spelling <c>fh-*</c> as a
    /// literal and a rename here breaking it silently; rendered from this
    /// constant and consumed from it, the link and the heading it lands on
    /// cannot drift apart.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// Stable across <see cref="HeadingLevel"/> — the level moves the tag,
    /// never the id — and renamed only as a deliberate breaking change to
    /// every host that links here, exactly like the file names on
    /// <see cref="SavedFiltersDocument"/>. The other eleven ids stay
    /// <c>internal</c>: no host links to them, and exporting them would
    /// freeze structure nobody depends on.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    public const string StorageSectionAnchorId = "fh-what-is-remembered";

    /// <summary>
    /// The storage-assurance section's heading text, for a host composing its
    /// own sentence around a link to <see cref="StorageSectionAnchorId"/> —
    /// the producer owns the phrase, the host owns the sentence it sits in
    /// (the <c>FolderPickDisplay.DeadPickVerdict</c> shape).
    /// <para>
    /// Exported beside the id rather than as a composed link so host phrasing
    /// stays the host's and no markup ends up inside a constant. The heading
    /// renders from this constant, so a reader following the link arrives at
    /// the words the link promised — and a reword is one edit, not two.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    public const string StorageSectionHeading = "What the panel remembers";

    /// <summary>
    /// The heading level of this block's lead heading ("Filters"); every
    /// section below it renders one level deeper. Valid values are 1 through
    /// 5 — a lead at <c>h6</c> would leave its sections nowhere to go.
    /// <para>
    /// A parameter because only the host knows the outline this block is
    /// embedded into, and only the host can keep that outline well-formed: a
    /// help page whose own sections are <c>h2</c> needs an <c>h3</c> lead
    /// here, while one embedded a tier deeper needs <c>h4</c>. The
    /// hard-coded <c>h4</c>/<c>h5</c> pair this replaced was right for
    /// nobody in particular — it skipped a level under the one host that
    /// existed, silently, because a skipped level is invisible on screen and
    /// shows up only in a screen reader's outline or an audit.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// Required, deliberately: a default would be a level this component
    /// cannot know is right, quietly wrong in every host whose outline
    /// differs from whichever one the default was chosen for.
    /// <see cref="EditorRequiredAttribute"/> makes an unstated level an
    /// <c>RZ2012</c> build error in the host, the same posture every other
    /// required parameter in this library takes — see the silent-splat entry
    /// in this subproject's Pitfalls. The range check below is the belt to
    /// that brace, covering the paths <c>RZ2012</c> cannot see (reflection,
    /// dynamic rendering, a test harness).
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    /// <exception cref="ArgumentOutOfRangeException">
    /// Thrown when the bound value is outside 1–5, including the unset
    /// default of zero.
    /// </exception>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public int HeadingLevel { get; set; }

    // The admissible lead levels. The upper bound is structural, not
    // stylistic: sections render at HeadingLevel + 1, and HTML has no h7.
    private const int MinHeadingLevel = 1;
    private const int MaxHeadingLevel = 5;

    // Every section heading sits exactly one level below the lead, so the
    // block contributes a well-formed two-tier outline wherever it lands.
    private int SectionLevel => HeadingLevel + 1;

    protected override void OnParametersSet()
    {
        if (HeadingLevel is < MinHeadingLevel or > MaxHeadingLevel)
        {
            throw new ArgumentOutOfRangeException(
                nameof(HeadingLevel), HeadingLevel,
                $"Heading level must be between {MinHeadingLevel} and {MaxHeadingLevel}: "
                + "this block renders a lead heading with its sections one level below, "
                + "so a lead at h6 would leave them nowhere to go.");
        }
    }

    // A heading whose tag is chosen at render time. Razor has no syntax for a
    // computed element name, and the alternative — emitting the tag through a
    // MarkupString — would hand raw markup to the renderer for text that is
    // otherwise escaped; building the frames keeps the id and the text
    // escaped exactly as literal markup would.
    private static RenderFragment Heading(int level, string id, string text) => builder =>
    {
        builder.OpenElement(0, "h" + level.ToString(CultureInfo.InvariantCulture));
        builder.AddAttribute(1, "id", id);
        builder.AddContent(2, text);
        builder.CloseElement();
    };
}
